the makeup of colombia's new congress  couldn't be more different than the  previous one for the first time in  history a progressive coalition is the  largest political bloc in both chambers  it's the result of unprecedented  elections that will bring a true leftist  gustavo petro in power in august  this is a historic congress for a number  of reasons first more than 60 percent of  representatives are new it's an  important renovation second it's diverse  with more indigenous representatives  that come from rural colombia and  finally for the first time 30 percent  are women this is very promising  but inauguration was fraught with  controversy outgoing president ivan duke  delivered a feisty speech defending his  accomplishments during his tenure  despite abysmal polling numbers around  20  he attacked the incoming majority saying  that they could put people's private  property at risk  it is possible to have social justice in  remote areas without expropriations and  prejudice and we offered an inflexible  compromise to peace with legality that  advanced in the territories  he was repeatedly interrupted by new  congress representatives they called him  a liar for saying he supported the  implementation of the peace deal and the  protection of social leaders who were  killed in unprecedented numbers during  duca's administration  the new congress is tasked with an  ambitious reform agenda to combat  poverty and inequality  this includes a major tax reform to fund  social programs and long postponed land  pension and police reforms  to do so petra has built a coalition  with centuries parties that should  assure him a majority  experts believe he will take advantage  of a honeymoon period few of his  predecessors had  for the first time we will have the  possibility of  getting through congress radical reforms  coming from the left  nevertheless given the fact that this is  a majority coalition and not a governing  coalition from the left wing party  those reforms will have to be tempered  down in order to be able to pass through  congress  the question now is just how long will  that honeymoon last given the fact that  the country is going through difficult  economic times with high inflation high  unemployment among other issue in an  electorate that's been demanding change  and reform quickly and surely alessandra  ampietti al jazeera bogota
 

